#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# subresultant_polynomial_remainders

#% text
# `subresultant_polynomial_remainders(p, q, x)` gives `p`, `q` and the subresultant polynomial remainder sequence of `p` and `q` in `x`.

#% text
# `subresultant_polynomial_remainders(p, q, x, modulus: n)` computes it modulo the prime `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# After `p` and `q` come the nonzero subresultants `Sj` that follow a regular one (of full degree `j + 1`), in decreasing degree: each is proportional to the next remainder of Euclid's algorithm, with coefficients that stay polynomial in the coefficients of the inputs.

#% item
# The last element is a greatest common divisor of `p` and `q` up to a factor free of `x`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
subresultant_polynomial_remainders(x^5 - x + 3, x^3 + 2*x - 1, x)
#% output
#= [3 - x + x^5, -1 + 2*x + x^3, -1 - 3*x - x^2, 2 + 10*x, -44]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
subresultant_polynomial_remainders((x - a)^2*(x + 1), (x - a)*(x - 1), x)
#% output
#= [x*a^2 - 2*a*x - 2*a*x^2 + a^2 + x^2 + x^3, a - x - a*x + x^2, -2*a + 2*x + 2*a^2 - 2*a*x]

#% cell
subresultant_polynomial_remainders(x^5 + x^2 + 1, x^3 + x + 1, x, modulus: 2)
#% output
#= [1 + x^2 + x^5, 1 + x + x^3, x, 1]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The last element divided by the gcd is free of `x`:

#% cell
cancel(last(subresultant_polynomial_remainders((x - 3)^2*(x + 1), (x - 3)*(x^2 + 1), x))/polynomial_gcd((x - 3)^2*(x + 1), (x - 3)*(x^2 + 1)))
#% output
#= 20

